# Basic-auth privacy gate for the Beevia status reports.
# Not hardened security — just keeps the reports off the open internet so
# only people with the shared password can read them.
#
# ── SETUP ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AuthUserFile is an absolute filesystem path — Apache cannot resolve one
# relative to this file. The path below is derived from deploy_staging.sh's
# REMOTE_PATH (/home/peytec5/beevia-management.peytechnologies.com), which
# rsyncs this folder's contents directly into that directory, so .htpasswd
# lands at its root rather than in a subfolder. If the deploy target ever
# changes, update REMOTE_PATH in deploy_staging.sh and this line together.
#
# ── ONE-CLICK LINKS (optional, no backend needed) ──────────────────────────
# Basic Auth accepts credentials embedded in the URL itself:
#   https://USERNAME:PASSWORD@yourdomain.com/path/to/web-report/
# The browser turns that into the same Authorization header a login prompt
# would send — nothing server-side changes. Percent-encode special
# characters in the password (@ -> %40, : -> %3A, etc.), and know that the
# link is the credential in plain text: readable in browser history, and
# visible to anyone the link is shared with (including chat apps that
# server-side fetch a preview). Fine for "a bit of privacy", not for
# anything that needs to stay secret from someone who has the link.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

AuthType Basic
AuthName "Beevia Project Status"
AuthUserFile /home/peytec5/beevia-management.peytechnologies.com/.htpasswd
Require valid-user

# Never serve the credentials file itself, however it's requested.
<FilesMatch "^\.ht">
    Require all denied
</FilesMatch>

# No need to expose the report folder listing.
Options -Indexes
